#{[#edb/ref 17592186049481 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065001} :activity/id #uuid "b866a7ca-c501-50dd-8117-0d7904f47708" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065001 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065405 :tutorial/content "An **antiderivative** is the *opposite* of a derivative. For example:\n\n- Using the power rule, we know that the *derivative* of $\\color{blue}x^2$ is ${\\color{red}2x}.$\n\n- Going in the *opposite* direction, we say that an **antiderivative** of $\\color{red}2x$ is ${\\color{blue}x^2}.$\n\nFor any given function, there are infinitely many antiderivatives. For example:\n\n- The derivative of $\\color{blue}x^2$ is ${\\color{red}2x},$ so an *antiderivative* of $\\color{red}2x$ is ${\\color{blue}x^2}.$\n\n- The derivative of $\\color{blue}x^2+1$ is ${\\color{red}2x},$ so another *antiderivative* of $\\color{red}2x$ is ${\\color{blue}x^2+1}.$\n\n- The derivative of $\\color{blue}x^2+2$ is ${\\color{red}2x},$ so another *antiderivative* of $\\color{red}2x$ is ${\\color{blue}x^2+2}.$\n\n- ...\n\nIn general, all the antiderivatives of $\\color{red}2x$ can be written in the form ${\\color{blue}x^2+C},$ where $C$ is a constant.\n\nTo represent the antiderivative symbolically, we use a special symbol $\\displaystyle \\int$ called the **integral** symbol. So, using **integral notation**, we can write\n\n$$\n\\int 2x \\, \\textrm d x = x^2 + C\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ represents an arbitrary constant and is called the **constant of integration**.\n\nIn words, $\\displaystyle \\int 2x \\, \\textrm d x$ is said as “the integral of $2x$ with respect to $x$” or “the antiderivative of $2x$.” Integrals like this are called **indefinite integrals**, and the function being **integrated** ($2x$ in this case) is called the **integrand**." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065154}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065002 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064167 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064038 :question/id #uuid "368cbbae-4eff-5eff-a8f3-d3e8359447a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-364" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int x^2 \\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=2$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int x^2 \\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac{x^{2+1}}{2+1} + C = \\dfrac{x^3}{3}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Monomial"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065003}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065003 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064168 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064039 :question/id #uuid "818f4fbe-e251-57bd-90ae-b6517c6f2f89" :question/math-academy-id "e-367" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of  $\\sqrt x.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the square root as a rational power, $\\sqrt x= x^{1/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{1/2+1}}{(\\frac{1}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2}}{(\\frac{3}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Radical Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065004}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065004 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064169 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064040 :question/id #uuid "c25e6037-3900-5314-b9a9-262fef07061c" :question/math-academy-id "e-368" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac 1 {x^3}$ and that $F'(x) = f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if $F'(x) = f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac 1 {x^3}= x^{-3}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3+1}}{-3+1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{-2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Reciprocal Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065005}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065005 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065406 :tutorial/content "Let's recap the main points:\n\n- Integrals like $\\displaystyle \\int x^3\\,\\textrm d x$ are called **indefinite integrals**.\n\n- Whenever we calculate an indefinite integral, we must always remember to add an **arbitrary constant** $+C.$\n\n- To integrate power functions, we can use the **power rule for integration**, which states that\n\n$$\n\\int x^n\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1} + C,\n$$\n\nprovided that $n \\neq -1.$ To emphasize, the power rule does *not* work when $n=-1.$ So we cannot use the above formula to work out $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x}\\,\\textrm d x.$ Integrals like this need to be handled differently, and you'll find out how soon." :tutorial/title "Some Final Notes on Indefinite Integrals and Constants"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065154 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065372 :tutorial/content "To compute the integral of any power function $x^n,$ where $n \\neq -1,$ we can use the **power rule for integration**. The power rule for integration states that\n\n$$\n\\int x^n\\, \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac {x^{n + 1}}{n + 1} + C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is any constant and $n\\neq -1.$ For example, in the case of $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{3+1}}{3+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the **antiderivative** of $f'(x) = x^3$ is $f(x) = \\dfrac {x^{4}}{4}+C .$ \n\nWe can check that this is the correct answer by taking the derivative of $f(x)$ and seeing that it is indeed equal to $x^3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{x}^{4})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4{x}^{4-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the constant $C$ disappeared when we differentiated because the derivative of any constant is zero. We always add $+C$ whenever we find the antiderivative of a function because we know that it will disappear when we differentiate.\n\n**Note:** The power rule for integration is just the power rule for differentiation in reverse!\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} \\left( x^{n+1} \\right) = (n+1)x^n \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad \\displaystyle \\int x^n \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}\n$$" :tutorial/title "The Power Rule for Integration"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065002}}] :activity/title "The Antiderivative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064406}] [#edb/ref 17592186048826 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074657} :activity/id #uuid "7809af08-83ac-5450-986d-2614a3fd22d3" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075033 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to solve, *without* using graphs, the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y=4 \\\\ x-2=y\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to solve the system without a graph, we will need to combine the equations into a single equation that can be solved using algebraic manipulation.\n\nOne way to combine the equations is called the **substitution method.** To use the substitution method, we substitute for one variable in terms of the other.\n\nFor instance, notice that we can make $y$ the subject of the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\color{red}y=x - 2\n$$\n\nSubstituting this expression for $y$ into the first equation, we get an equation that we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =4 \\\\ x+(x-2) & =4 \\\\ 2x-2 & =4 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=3.$ But we also need to figure out what $y$ is. We can do this by substituting $x=3$ back into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x-2 \\\\ y & =3-2 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x = 3$ and $y = 1$, which can be written as $\\left(3, 1\\right).$\n\n**Note:** Whenever we isolate the variable in one equation, we have to substitute it into the *other* equation. We cannot substitute a variable into the same equation it came from, because this will not give us any more information about the variable." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074658}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073752 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073445 :question/id #uuid "e9aae52c-65f3-5dff-bc6b-a530f8c7acc1" :question/math-academy-id "e-13009" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=4 \\\\ x+2y=16\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the first equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $x$ by adding $y$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =4 \\\\ x & =4+y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we replace $x$ with $4+y$ in the second equation and solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\tx+2y &=16 \\\n\t(4+y) + 2y &=16\\\n\t4+3y &=16\\\n\t3y &=12\\\n\ty &=4\n}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $y=4$ into $x=4+y$, then we can find the value of $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\tx &= 4 + y \\\n\t   &= 4 + (4) \\\n\t   &=8\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x = 8$ and $y = 4$, which can be written as $(8, 4).$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Linear Equations Using Substitution"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074659}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074659 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073753 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073446 :question/id #uuid "707504e8-a367-5dc2-a663-371a9dcf7224" :question/math-academy-id "e-13010" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2u+9v-6=0 \\\\ 2u+6v-8=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n![](images/77/77459b19bb1fd1f28e05e6f613e4e49af34595eae33eeeca584d65a289f7ea2c.svg)\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $u$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t2u+6v-8 &= 0 \\\n2u+6v &= 8 \\\n2u &= 8 - 6v \\\nu &= \\dfrac{8-6v}{2} \\\nu &= 4 - 3v\n}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $u=4-3v$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $v\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\n\t2(4-3v)+9v-6 &= 0 \\\n\t8-6v+9v-6 &= 0 \\\n2+3v &= 0 \\\n3v &= -2 \\\nv &= -\\dfrac{2}{3}\n}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $v=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $u\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\n\t2u+9\\left(-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right) -6 &= 0 \\\n\t2u-6-6 &= 0 \\\n\t2u-12 &= 0 \\\n\t2u &= 12 \\\n\tu &= 6\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=6$ and $v=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$, which can be written as $\\left(6,-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right).$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Challenging System of Linear Equations Using Substitution"}}] :activity/title "Solving Systems of Equations by Substitution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073923}] [#edb/ref 17592186049715 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097478} :activity/id #uuid "fe9cb489-c107-59bc-888d-07dcb3a33049" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097478 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097832 :tutorial/content "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ shown in the picture below. The vector $2\\mathbf{a}$ is the vector that has the same direction as $\\mathbf{a}$ and a length (magnitude) that is twice the length of $\\mathbf{a}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}2\\mathbf{a} &= 2 \\cdot & \\mathbf{a}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/01/01c393b2df3d39e4f62faca475a26ea835e1ab88266628509d58e21edd36c79b.png)\n\nOn the other hand, $-1.5\\mathbf{a}$ is the vector that has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{a}$ and its magnitude is $1.5$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}-1.5\\mathbf{a} &= 1.5 \\cdot & \\mathbf{a}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/73/73ea353af74ab9518ea7a963985ad26ad36723c7e3a335391628f1eba208139e.png)\n\nNotice that $2\\mathbf{a}$ and $-1.5\\mathbf{a}$ are both parallel to $\\mathbf a$, and are parallel to each other." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097480}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097479 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096648 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096445 :question/id #uuid "069b2882-f4a9-589f-a903-4e89d077cd85" :question/math-academy-id "e-4279" :question/problem "Suppose, $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ and $\\mathbf{b} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$. Given that $7\\mathbf{a}+14\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\lambda \\left(3\\mathbf{a}+6\\mathbf{b} \\right)$ are equal vectors, find the scalar factor $\\lambda$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\n7\\mathbf{a}+14\\mathbf{b}= \\dfrac{7}{3} (3\\mathbf{a}+6\\mathbf{b})\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lambda=\\dfrac{7}{3}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Vectors are Parallel"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097563}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097480 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096649 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096446 :question/id #uuid "d1329498-4413-5d87-a814-be0d57a3afd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4280" :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf a$ is shown in the diagram below. Draw the vectors $2\\mathbf a$ and $-3\\mathbf a$.\n\n![](images/a5/a5eaa8baaf303ecbfa6c0fb207d6b19836cc90fc331e238cd473109f44fddf80.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that both $2\\mathbf{a}$ and $-3\\mathbf{a}$ must be parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nNow, for the vector $2\\mathbf{a}$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $2\\mathbf{a}$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $2\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction since the factor $2$ is positive.\n\n![](images/41/41eba7e6c5bbe420418726bd45efa668dc6be5ff5144ba35d52b53a43925c333.png)\n\nNext, for the vector $-3\\mathbf{a}$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $3\\mathbf{a}$ is $3$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $-3\\mathbf{a}$ have opposite directions since the factor $-3$ is negative.\n\n![](images/ed/eda3f2572d98ef1789292ec6d35569487b32efa459673dcb95f8fdd0d1f4ae5b.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Creating Parallel Vectors"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097562}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097481 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097834 :tutorial/content "For any vector $\\mathbf{a}$ the vector $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{a}$ is equal to the zero vector:\n\n$$\n0\\cdot\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nThis means that the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ is parallel to *any* vector." :tutorial/title "The Zero Vector is Parallel to Any Vector"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097562 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097852 :tutorial/content "In general, two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are called **parallel** (or **collinear**) if there exists a number $\\lambda$ such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\lambda \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nSometimes, we'll use a special notation\n$\\mathbf{a} \\parallel \\mathbf{b}$\nto denote that $\\mathbf{a}$ is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$." :tutorial/title "Determining Whether Two Vectors Are Parallel"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097479}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096700 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096494 :question/id #uuid "11c09a26-0e83-5a77-b777-aea8a3594960" :question/math-academy-id "e-5183" :question/problem "Consider the diagram that is shown below. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{b}=-\\mathbf{c}$\n2. $\\mathbf{e}=2\\mathbf{a}$\n3. $\\mathbf{a}=2\\mathbf{e}$\n\n![](images/95/95ae711c1ccbf7543e6d269aa8d817a66e2ac348113ea71f3a1babcbb23d1ca3.png)" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false. The vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ have different magnitudes. So, $\\mathbf{b} \\neq -\\mathbf{c}$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{e}$ are parallel and have the same direction. Also, the magnitude of $\\mathbf{e}$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$. So, $\\mathbf{e}=2\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Statement III is false. The magnitude of $\\mathbf{e}$ is greater than the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$. So, $\\mathbf{a} \\neq 2\\mathbf{e}$.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parallel Vectors From a Diagram"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097481}}] :activity/title "Parallel Vectors" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096883}] [#edb/ref 17592186049360 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097737} :activity/id #uuid "886871c8-0b6e-56ce-bc7f-54affec4fd88" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097737 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097833 :tutorial/content "A **unit vector** is a vector whose magnitude is equal to $1$.\n\nGiven any vector $\\mathbf a$, we can create a unit vector $\\mathbf u$ by dividing $\\mathbf a$ by its magnitude $\\mid \\, \\mathbf{a} \\, \\mid$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid}\n$$\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\n![](images/c7/c78d5b75c1d5928e7b269a4a67229487b3d3324e9815b11c5fd1f47f63437d82.png)" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097738}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097738 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096813 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096447 :question/id #uuid "2a61b115-4e26-5966-b2a9-d2fedef6b728" :question/math-academy-id "e-4282" :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ has magnitude $3$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$." :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$, we divide $\\mathbf{a}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\mathbf{a}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097739}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097739 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096814 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096493 :question/id #uuid "6dfe09f0-30e2-53bc-a8f6-d7e4a3b8232a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5181" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d3042242136b82b867b8bd7911761ee38318924df0c9979488d20f04117c45d9.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf a$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/b3/b316448c885a20a7ed60871ce0f1a4dc4f77bd4658dc6eda3407fb3c89a088cb.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have\n\n$$\n\\angle AHB = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\n, $AH=3$ and $BH=2$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^{2} + BH^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3^{2} + 2^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9 + 4} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{13}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$ is $\\sqrt{13}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{\\mid a \\mid} \\cdot a \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector in a Diagram"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097740}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097740 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096815 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096448 :question/id #uuid "23d9a601-974b-51c2-a6ea-0ebadde19efc" :question/math-academy-id "e-4283" :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude $10$, while the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is directed vertically down and has magnitude $20$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a+b}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $\\mathbf{a+b}$. According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$ goes from the tail of $\\mathbf{a}$ to the head of $\\mathbf{b}$:\n\n![](images/32/32c48d8c19fc68fbc172b240ded922f3e2ebc12804411c318ff63fe999aaedcc.png)\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $\\mathbf{a+b}$, we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{a + b}{\\mid a + b \\mid}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, first notice that we obtain a right triangle. So, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a + b & ^{2} &= & a & ^{2} +\\left.\\end{vmatrix}b\\right|^{2} \\\\\n&= 10^{2} + 20^{2} \\\\\n&= 100 + 400 \\\\\n&= 500\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{a+b} &= \\sqrt{500}=10\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{a + b}{\\mid a + b \\mid} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10\\sqrt{5}}(a + b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Vector Sum"}}] :activity/title "Unit Vectors" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096884}]}
